---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import Tag from "../../components/docs/Tag.astro";

// Follows docs/integrations/ai-gateway.md and packages/coding.sh/src/integrations/gateway (providers.ts lists the gateways).
const claude = `// ~/.claude/settings.json
{ "env": {
  "ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL": "https://ai-gateway.vercel.sh/claude-code",
  "ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN": "<your AI Gateway key>",
  "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY": ""
} }`;

const codex = `# ~/.codex/config.toml
model_provider = "vercel"
model = "openai/gpt-5"

[model_providers.vercel]
name = "Vercel AI Gateway"
base_url = "https://ai-gateway.vercel.sh/codex/v1"
env_key = "AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY"
wire_api = "responses"`;

const aiSdk = `const { text } = await generateText({
  model: "openai/gpt-5", // any creator/model, billed through the gateway
  prompt,
  experimental_telemetry: { isEnabled: true },
});`;

const sdk = `// TypeScript
const openai = observe(new OpenAI({ baseURL: "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1", apiKey: process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY }));

# Python
client = coding_sh.observe(OpenAI(base_url="https://gateway.ai.cloudflare.com/v1/<account>/<gateway>/compat"))`;

const cmds = `coding.sh gateway status          # which agents route through a gateway, keys set or not
coding.sh gateway enrich          # billed cost + serving provider per call (last 30 days)
coding.sh sessions --flag gateway:openrouter
coding.sh compare sonnet gpt5 --gateway --k 3
coding.sh compare sonnet gpt5 --gateway openrouter`;

const config = `# ~/.coding.sh/config.toml (only needed to change the defaults)
[gateway]
default = "openrouter"            # what a bare compare --gateway uses

[gateway.litellm]
baseUrl = "http://localhost:4000"  # or LITELLM_PROXY_API_BASE

[gateway.cloudflare]
accountId = "<32 hex>"             # or CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
gatewayId = "prod"`;

const GATEWAY_ROWS = [
  { name: "Vercel AI Gateway", enrich: "/v1/generation", compare: "vercel", key: "AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY" },
  { name: "OpenRouter", enrich: "/api/v1/generation", compare: "openrouter", key: "OPENROUTER_API_KEY" },
  { name: "LiteLLM proxy", enrich: "/spend/logs", compare: "litellm", key: "LITELLM_PROXY_API_KEY" },
  { name: "Cloudflare AI Gateway", enrich: "logs API", compare: "", key: "CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN" },
  { name: "Portkey", enrich: "/v1/logs", compare: "", key: "PORTKEY_API_KEY" },
  { name: "Helicone", enrich: "/v1/request", compare: "", key: "HELICONE_API_KEY" },
  { name: "Ramp Router (router.com)", enrich: "cost from the response", compare: "ramp", key: "RAMP_ROUTER_API_KEY" },
  { name: "Requesty", enrich: "cost from the response", compare: "", key: "REQUESTY_API_KEY" },
  { name: "TrueFoundry, Kong, Bifrost, Martian", enrich: "", compare: "", key: "" },
  { name: "CLIProxyAPI", enrich: "usage queue (--gateway cliproxyapi)", compare: "", key: "CLIPROXYAPI_MANAGEMENT_KEY" },
];
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    An LLM gateway gives you one key for many models. coding.sh tags sessions that went through one, adds what each call cost and which
    provider served it, and runs A/B experiments across models with that one key.
  </p>

  <h2 id="supported">Supported gateways</h2>
  <div class="doc-table doc-table--stack">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Gateway</th>
          <th><code>gateway enrich</code></th>
          <th><code>compare --gateway</code></th>
          <th>Key</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          GATEWAY_ROWS.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <strong>{g.name}</strong>
              </td>
              <td data-label="gateway enrich">{!g.enrich ? "detect only" : g.enrich.startsWith("/") ? <code>{g.enrich}</code> : g.enrich}</td>
              <td data-label="compare --gateway">{g.compare ? <code>{g.compare}</code> : "no"}</td>
              <td data-label="Key">{g.key ? <code>{g.key}</code> : ""}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every gateway in the table is detected: sessions get a <code>gateway:&lt;name&gt;</code> tag. Keys come from the standard variable, or
    from a <code>[gateway.&lt;name&gt;]</code> table:
  </p>
  <Code title="toml" code={config} />

  <h2 id="setup">Route your agent</h2>
  <p>Point the agent's base URL at the gateway. The examples below use Vercel AI Gateway with <code>AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY</code>. For OpenRouter, use <code>https://openrouter.ai/api</code> as Claude Code's base URL and your OpenRouter key.</p>
  <h3 id="claude-code">Claude Code</h3>
  <Code title="json" code={claude} />
  <h3 id="codex">Codex</h3>
  <Code title="toml" code={codex} />
  <h3 id="ai-sdk">AI SDK</h3>
  <p>Send its telemetry to <code>coding.sh collector</code>, as on the <a href="/docs/otel">OpenTelemetry</a> page.</p>
  <Code title="ts" code={aiSdk} />
  <p>OpenCode uses its built-in <code>vercel</code> or <code>openrouter</code> provider, and Pi uses <code>vercel-ai-gateway</code> or <code>openrouter</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="use">Use it <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <Code code={cmds} />
  <p>
    Detection is read-only. coding.sh never prints or stores your keys. Re-running <code>enrich</code> makes no new API calls. OpenRouter
    and Requesty calls link by their response id. LiteLLM calls link through its OpenTelemetry export. Cloudflare, Portkey and Helicone
    need the log id their response header carries: the SDKs record it, agents don't keep it. Calls through a Vercel <code>vmc/</code> virtual model record
    the model it resolved to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sdk">From the SDKs <Tag status="built" /></h2>
  <p>
    Apps traced with the <a href="/docs/sdk">TypeScript or Python SDK</a> detect every gateway in the table on each model call: by base
    URL (a self-hosted gateway by its URL env var, like <code>LITELLM_PROXY_API_BASE</code>), by a response header only that gateway sets,
    or by the response id. The call records the gateway and its request id, so the session is tagged and <code>gateway enrich</code> can
    look it up.
  </p>
  <Code code={sdk} />
  <p>
    Request ids come from <code>cf-aig-log-id</code> (Cloudflare), <code>helicone-id</code>, <code>x-litellm-call-id</code>,
    <code>x-portkey-trace-id</code> and <code>x-kong-request-id</code>, or the response id (<code>gen_…</code> Vercel,
    <code>gen-…</code> OpenRouter, <code>rqsty-…</code> Requesty). The SDKs read headers only, never a body twice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="drains">Trace drains <Tag status="partial" /></h2>
  <p>
    Vercel Pro and Enterprise plans can drain one OpenTelemetry trace per gateway request. Tunnel to <code>coding.sh collector</code> and use
    <code>&lt;tunnel&gt;/v1/traces</code> as the drain URL. Each request becomes one model call with its billed cost. Failovers are not
    counted as extra calls. <code>enrich</code> then links each request to the agent session that made it. LiteLLM, Cloudflare, Portkey,
    OpenRouter (Broadcast), TrueFoundry and Bifrost exports are tagged with their gateway and cost.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
